  "name": "Confirmation of a Convoluted Flow over the Southeastern Bering Sea Shelf",
  "description": "Data from 16 CTD sections during spring 1979-1981 were used to derive mean geopotential topography and geostrophic flow (and its variability). Inshore of 100 m, there was a weak (~1.5 cm s) but quite stable southeastward flow, with a horizontal extent of at least 60 km. Offshore of 100 m, an even wider northwestward flow of 4-6 cm s was present, with a suggestion of anticyclonic eddies in the region ~20% of the time. Inshore of 100 m, however, eddy activity was quite rare. The main features from the synoptic data are virtually identical to those in a recent study using spatially averaged climatological data. [ Reference: Reed, R.K. (1998): Confirmation of a convoluted flow over the southeastern Bering Sea shelf. Cont. Shelf. Res., 18, 99 - 103. ]\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: A report showing the confirmation of a convoluted flow over the southeastern Bering Sea shelf",
  "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/23564485",
